{#- SUBSTITUTE TEMPLATE for a Llama-3.0-era checkpoint that ships no chat template and whose
    tokenizer lacks the Llama-3.1 tool tokens (<|python_tag|>, <|eom_id|>).

    WHY IT EXISTS. aaditya/Llama3-OpenBioLLM-8B is a Llama-3.0 fine-tune: max_position_embeddings
    8192, no chat_template in tokenizer_config.json, and 256 added tokens of which NONE is
    <|python_tag|>. It was being served with --tool-call-parser llama3_json and with llama.jinja
    (Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct's own template, which emits <|python_tag|>). vLLM answered every request
    with HTTP 500: "Llama3JsonToolParser could not locate the bot token '<|python_tag|>' in the
    tokenizer." vLLM's own documentation says llama3_json targets Llama 3.1/3.2/4 and that "no
    parser exists for Llama 3.0-era models without 3.1+ tool tokens".

    So the format has to be taught in the prompt and parsed from plain text. This template renders
    the Llama-3 header format the checkpoint WAS trained on, and asks for Hermes-style tool calls --
    <tool_call>{"name": ..., "arguments": {...}}</tool_call> -- which the `hermes` parser reads out
    of ordinary text with no special-token prerequisite at all.

    DISCLOSURE. A row produced this way answers "can this model be driven through a tool loop in a
    format it was never trained on", not "does this model do tool use". gen/newrun.sh records this
    path in every run's config.yaml so such rows are identifiable and must not be pooled with rows
    served on a native tool format.

    Two traps this template is written to avoid, both paid for already:
      * the sandboxed Jinja vLLM renders in forbids list.append() and has no `fromjson` filter;
      * a placeholder like [tool_name(argument="value")] gets COPIED LITERALLY (MedGemma 1.5 emitted
        a tool actually named `tool`), so the instructions below name the REAL tools by iterating
        over `tools` and never show a fake one.
-#}
{{- bos_token }}
{%- set ns = namespace(system="") %}
{%- for m in messages %}
    {%- if m['role'] == 'system' %}
        {%- set ns.system = m['content'] %}
    {%- endif %}
{%- endfor %}
<|start_header_id|>system<|end_header_id|>

{{ ns.system }}
{%- if tools %}

## Available tools
{% for t in tools %}
- {{ (t.function.name if t.function is defined else t.name) }}: {{ (t.function.description if t.function is defined else t.description) }}
  arguments schema: {{ (t.function.parameters if t.function is defined else t.parameters) | tojson }}
{% endfor %}
{#- ORDER MATTERS, and it cost a probe. The first version of this block put the format instruction
    BEFORE the tool list, so the last thing the model saw was a list -- and openbio_8b simply
    continued it, emitting "- cw_move: Take a move by its id. ... arguments schema: {...}" as its
    reply and scoring 0/5 on the tool probe. The imperative goes LAST. -#}
## How to answer
Reply with exactly one line, and nothing else:

<tool_call>{"name": "NAME", "arguments": {ARGUMENTS}}</tool_call>

{%- set ns2 = namespace(n='') %}{%- for t in tools %}{%- set ns2.n = ns2.n + (', ' if ns2.n else '') + (t.function.name if t.function is defined else t.name) %}{%- endfor %}
where NAME is one of {{ ns2.n }} and ARGUMENTS is a
JSON object matching that tool's schema above. Do not repeat the tool list. Do not explain. Emit the
single <tool_call> line.
{%- endif %}<|eot_id|>
{%- for m in messages %}
    {%- if m['role'] == 'system' %}
    {%- elif m['role'] == 'tool' %}
{#- Llama 3.0 has no `ipython` role, so a tool RESULT is delivered as a user turn. #}
<|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>

<tool_response>{{ m['content'] }}</tool_response><|eot_id|>
    {%- else %}
<|start_header_id|>{{ m['role'] }}<|end_header_id|>

{{ m['content'] if m['content'] is not none else "" }}
        {%- if m['tool_calls'] %}
            {%- for c in m['tool_calls'] %}
<tool_call>{"name": "{{ c.function.name }}", "arguments": {{ c.function.arguments }}}</tool_call>
            {%- endfor %}
        {%- endif %}<|eot_id|>
    {%- endif %}
{%- endfor %}
{%- if add_generation_prompt %}
<|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>

{% endif %}
